1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,067
Hi — I'm Divij Pawar. Here's a problem I ran into: an

2
00:00:03,067 --> 00:00:06,133
AI system gave a conclusion with confidence and a good explanation.

3
00:00:06,733 --> 00:00:09,600
But what if that explanation was completely made up? What if the

4
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:13,533
AI just hallucinated a reason that sounded right? An unauditable conclusion is

5
00:00:13,533 --> 00:00:17,000
a system failure, no matter how accurate it actually is. I built

6
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:19,100
a layer to stop that from happening silently.

7
00:00:19,930 --> 00:00:22,597
I built a layer that sits between any AI system and the

8
00:00:22,597 --> 00:00:23,930
person trusting its answer.

9
00:00:24,497 --> 00:00:27,163
It doesn't make the AI more accurate — that's a different problem.

10
00:00:27,530 --> 00:00:30,697
Instead, it makes it structurally impossible for a conclusion to sneak through

11
00:00:30,697 --> 00:00:31,863
without proof behind it.

12
00:00:32,230 --> 00:00:35,763
The layer enforces three things: every conclusion must be sourced, the record

13
00:00:35,763 --> 00:00:39,497
must be permanent and unchangeable, and if anything breaks, the whole pipeline

14
00:00:39,497 --> 00:00:40,797
stops instead of guessing.

15
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:44,227
Imagine you have four AI agents analyzing a company.

16
00:00:44,593 --> 00:00:45,793
One agent looks at finances.

17
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:48,693
Another checks patents. A third analyzes earnings.

18
00:00:49,060 --> 00:00:52,293
The fourth evaluates competition. They all send their findings to a final

19
00:00:52,293 --> 00:00:55,993
system that combines them into a single recommendation: buy, hold, or sell.

20
00:00:56,693 --> 00:00:59,893
Now imagine the financial agent's analysis is based on a number it

21
00:00:59,893 --> 00:01:03,927
made up — a hallucinated value that never actually existed. Nothing stops

22
00:01:03,927 --> 00:01:04,727
it.

23
00:01:04,593 --> 00:01:07,527
That fake number flows right through the system and becomes part of

24
00:01:07,527 --> 00:01:08,860
an official investment decision.

25
00:01:09,227 --> 00:01:12,593
And here's the scary part: AI systems are really good at explaining

26
00:01:12,593 --> 00:01:13,427
wrong answers.

27
00:01:13,893 --> 00:01:16,627
Ask an AI why it made a mistake, and it will invent

28
00:01:16,627 --> 00:01:21,027
a plausible, confident explanation for something that never actually happened inside

29
00:01:21,027 --> 00:01:21,827
the model.

30
00:01:22,250 --> 00:01:24,883
I looked at two other ways to solve this, and rejected both.

31
00:01:25,317 --> 00:01:28,917
First: use another AI to check the first AI's reasoning. That doesn't

32
00:01:28,917 --> 00:01:31,850
work, because the checking AI has the same blind spot as the

33
00:01:31,850 --> 00:01:35,817
one being checked. A perfectly crafted, completely fake explanation can sound just

34
00:01:35,817 --> 00:01:37,617
as genuine to a checker as a real one.

35
00:01:38,083 --> 00:01:42,050
Second: mathematically trace through the AI's computation to verify the reasoning.

36
00:01:42,517 --> 00:01:43,350
Theoretically sound,

37
00:01:43,783 --> 00:01:47,350
but it forces you to abandon powerful AI models and use much

38
00:01:47,350 --> 00:01:50,217
weaker ones. Neither solution actually worked.

39
00:01:50,790 --> 00:01:52,323
So I built three different mechanisms.

40
00:01:52,723 --> 00:01:53,523
First: a

41
00:01:53,757 --> 00:01:56,757
ReasoningObject. Before any conclusion is allowed to propagate — before it reaches

42
00:01:56,757 --> 00:01:59,457
an investor or gets acted on — the AI must produce a

43
00:01:59,457 --> 00:02:00,423
signed affidavit.

44
00:02:00,823 --> 00:02:03,323
What did you conclude? How confident are you? What was the exact

45
00:02:03,323 --> 00:02:06,157
source line that led you there? What citations support this?

46
00:02:06,557 --> 00:02:09,523
If the AI can't point to the actual source that justified its

47
00:02:09,523 --> 00:02:12,223
conclusion, the state transition is rejected outright.

48
00:02:12,923 --> 00:02:13,723
Second:

49
00:02:13,757 --> 00:02:16,957
append-only checkpointing. Every record gets written to a database in a way

50
00:02:16,957 --> 00:02:18,690
that prevents updates or deletions.

51
00:02:19,157 --> 00:02:22,723
This is enforced by the database itself, not by application code, because

52
00:02:22,723 --> 00:02:25,890
code can have bugs or get bypassed. If a reviewer later flags

53
00:02:25,890 --> 00:02:28,623
a run as wrong, that flag goes into a separate table. The

54
00:02:28,623 --> 00:02:32,023
original record is never touched. You can always reconstruct exactly what the

55
00:02:32,023 --> 00:02:33,490
system saw and said at the time.

56
00:02:34,023 --> 00:02:35,757
Third: Adversarial Arbitration.

57
00:02:36,323 --> 00:02:40,057
When two agents genuinely disagree on something important, they debate once.

58
00:02:40,690 --> 00:02:44,923
If they still disagree, that disagreement becomes a first-class output — an

59
00:02:44,923 --> 00:02:48,623
Affidavit of Disagreement — instead of being quietly averaged away into a

60
00:02:48,623 --> 00:02:49,757
meaningless middle ground.

61
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:52,000
Here's where theory meets reality.

62
00:02:52,433 --> 00:02:54,733
I needed to force the AI to structure its output in a

63
00:02:54,733 --> 00:02:58,233
specific way: two XML blocks, a thought log and a conclusion, nothing

64
00:02:58,233 --> 00:02:59,033
else.

65
00:02:59,133 --> 00:03:01,867
Version one was a polite request — 'please format your output like

66
00:03:01,867 --> 00:03:04,667
this.' I tested it against live Claude output. It failed.

67
00:03:05,067 --> 00:03:07,700
The model wrote a numbered preamble before the opening tag, and my

68
00:03:07,700 --> 00:03:10,800
parser couldn't find the blocks. So the fix wasn't a softer prompt

69
00:03:10,867 --> 00:03:12,200
— it was a mechanical constraint.

70
00:03:12,867 --> 00:03:15,967
First character must be a bracket. No preamble allowed. Period.

71
00:03:16,367 --> 00:03:19,500
Now when parsing fails, it logs immediately as a PARSE_FAILURE with the

72
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:20,567
broken response attached.

73
00:03:21,233 --> 00:03:24,733
One retry happens with an explicit corrective directive. If that fails too,

74
00:03:24,767 --> 00:03:28,433
the entire pipeline halts. No grade is delivered. And here's the key:

75
00:03:28,667 --> 00:03:31,367
both the failure and the success are part of history, not just

76
00:03:31,367 --> 00:03:32,167
the success.

77
00:03:32,630 --> 00:03:34,397
Now here's the part I need to say clearly.

78
00:03:34,763 --> 00:03:37,997
Everything I just showed you enforces structure. It guarantees a thought log

79
00:03:37,997 --> 00:03:41,763
exists. It guarantees it's well-formed. It guarantees it's permanently recorded.

80
00:03:42,163 --> 00:03:44,797
But it does NOT guarantee that the log reflects what actually happened

81
00:03:44,797 --> 00:03:45,697
inside the AI.

82
00:03:46,097 --> 00:03:49,530
A hallucinated conclusion can be accompanied by a perfectly coherent, perfectly

83
00:03:49,530 --> 00:03:53,797
formatted, completely fabricated thought log. That log will pass every structural

84
00:03:53,797 --> 00:03:54,630
check I've built.

85
00:03:55,230 --> 00:03:58,097
Every single one. I wrote this down explicitly — we call it

86
00:03:58,097 --> 00:04:01,997
ADR-06 in our design log: the log is evidence of output, not

87
00:04:01,997 --> 00:04:02,963
evidence of process.

88
00:04:03,463 --> 00:04:05,697
This is not a bug I plan to fix later. It's a

89
00:04:05,697 --> 00:04:09,297
fundamental property of how large language models work. No logging layer, by

90
00:04:09,297 --> 00:04:10,463
itself, can solve this.

91
00:04:10,863 --> 00:04:14,097
I see a lot of 'AI accountability' framing that quietly slides past

92
00:04:14,097 --> 00:04:14,930
this distinction.

93
00:04:15,297 --> 00:04:17,897
It presents a structured trace as if structure equals truth.

94
00:04:18,530 --> 00:04:21,863
I'd rather say clearly what this system is and isn't. It's not

95
00:04:21,863 --> 00:04:22,663
a

96
00:04:22,530 --> 00:04:24,363
hallucination-prevention system. It's not a

97
00:04:24,730 --> 00:04:28,963
fact-verification system. It's a structural enforcement system with a known, honest

98
00:04:28,963 --> 00:04:29,763
limit.

99
00:04:30,080 --> 00:04:33,113
So where does that leave us? Structural enforcement is real and tested.

100
00:04:33,413 --> 00:04:36,847
The three mechanisms I built work. Ground-truth checking is real, where a

101
00:04:36,847 --> 00:04:39,513
ground truth exists to check against — like comparing what an AI

102
00:04:39,513 --> 00:04:42,913
claims about a financial filing to the actual filing from the SEC.

103
00:04:43,813 --> 00:04:47,080
Reproducibility checking is real — run the same request twice and look

104
00:04:47,080 --> 00:04:49,713
for numbers that appear in only one of the two runs. And

105
00:04:49,713 --> 00:04:53,047
the hardest problem — proving that the stated reasoning is the actual

106
00:04:53,047 --> 00:04:56,180
reasoning — that one remains genuinely open, and it might not be

107
00:04:56,180 --> 00:05:00,080
fully solvable with current AI models. But here's what I can guarantee:

108
00:05:00,447 --> 00:05:04,447
when structural accountability fails, it fails loudly, on the record, instead of

109
00:05:04,447 --> 00:05:06,713
quietly becoming someone's investment decision.

110
00:05:07,247 --> 00:05:08,747
That's the difference this layer makes.

111
00:05:09,500 --> 00:05:12,600
Here's a challenge for you: design a validation directive for your own

112
00:05:12,600 --> 00:05:16,267
AI pipeline that fails loudly instead of quietly accepting a bad answer.

113
00:05:16,933 --> 00:05:18,267
When you build it, check three things.

114
00:05:18,700 --> 00:05:21,533
First: does it log the failure immediately, with the full broken response

115
00:05:21,533 --> 00:05:22,333
attached?

116
00:05:22,433 --> 00:05:24,967
Second: does the retry get its own separate record in the system?

117
00:05:25,333 --> 00:05:26,133
Third:

118
00:05:25,833 --> 00:05:28,167
does it halt the pipeline on a second failure instead of just

119
00:05:28,167 --> 00:05:30,667
guessing and moving on? If any of those three are missing,

120
00:05:31,067 --> 00:05:33,067
you've found your accountability gap.

121
00:05:33,590 --> 00:05:34,390
Signing off — Divij Pawar.
